  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Landscape lighting cost is the first question nearly every homeowner asks, and the honest answer is a range rather than a number. A professionally installed 12V low-voltage LED system in Austin typically falls between 2,500 and 6,500 dollars for a front yard and between 6,000 and 20,000 dollars or more for a full property, depending on fixture count, fixture quality, terrain, and controls. Those are planning ranges based on what Austin Landscape Lighting sees across more than 500 local projects, not quotes. This guide breaks the total into its parts, shows what small, medium, and large projects usually include, and explains the factors that move the number so you can budget with confidence before anyone walks your yard.</p>

<h2>Why Landscape Lighting Is Priced the Way It Is</h2>
<p>A landscape lighting system has five cost components: fixtures, the transformer and controls, cable and connectors, design and installation labor, and any site-specific extras such as trenching under hardscape or climbing for moonlighting. Fixtures and labor dominate. On most residential projects, fixtures make up 40 to 50 percent of the total, labor 30 to 40 percent, and the transformer, cable, and controls the remainder.</p>
<p>Because the system is modular, cost scales almost linearly with fixture count. That makes per-fixture installed pricing a useful planning tool. In the Austin market, professionally installed brass or copper LED fixtures generally land between 250 and 450 dollars each all-in, including the share of transformer, cable, and labor. Premium specialty fixtures and difficult placements push higher.</p>

<h2>Cost by Component</h2>
<h3>Fixtures</h3>
<p>Fixture quality is the biggest variable. Solid brass or copper path lights, uplights, and well lights with integrated or interchangeable LED lamps run 90 to 250 dollars each for the hardware alone. Powder-coated aluminum fixtures cost 40 to 120 dollars but typically fail within three to six years in Austin's heat and sprinkler scale, so most professionals do not install them. Specialty items such as hardscape lights for retaining wall caps, recessed step lights, and submersible pool or fountain fixtures range from 120 to 350 dollars.</p>
<h3>Transformer and controls</h3>
<p>A quality multi-tap magnetic transformer in the 150 to 300 watt range costs 250 to 600 dollars. A built-in photocell and astronomical timer adds 40 to 120 dollars. Smart controllers that bring zone control, dimming, scenes, and app or voice control add 300 to 900 dollars depending on how many zones the property needs. Larger estates with two or three transformers multiply this line.</p>
<h3>Cable and connectors</h3>
<p>Direct-burial 12-gauge cable runs roughly 1 to 1.50 dollars per foot, and 10-gauge for long runs about 1.75 to 2.50 dollars. A typical front yard uses 200 to 400 feet. Gel-filled waterproof connectors add 3 to 6 dollars per splice. Cable rarely exceeds 10 percent of a project's total.</p>
<h3>Design and labor</h3>
<p>Installation labor for a trained two-person crew in Austin generally runs 85 to 150 dollars per hour, and most residential systems install in a single day. Design time is often included in the installed price by full-service companies. Standalone lighting design, if you want a plan to bid out or install yourself, runs 300 to 1,200 dollars depending on property size.</p>
<h3>Site extras</h3>
<p>Boring under a driveway or sidewalk adds 150 to 400 dollars per crossing. Rocky Hill Country soil in Westlake, Lakeway, and Bee Cave slows trenching and can add a few hundred dollars. Moonlighting that requires climbing or a lift to mount fixtures 25 feet up in a live oak adds 100 to 250 dollars per fixture. A new GFCI outlet, if one is not available near the transformer location, requires an electrician at 200 to 450 dollars.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/landscape-lighting-installation-austin-tx-2.webp" alt="Open trench with low-voltage cable and gel-filled connectors beside a brass path light" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Cable and connectors are a small slice of total cost, but quality direct-burial wire and waterproof splices prevent the failures that lead to repair bills.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Planning Ranges by Project Size</h2>
<p>The table below groups typical Austin projects into three tiers. Every figure is a planning range for professionally installed brass or copper LED systems. Your property may land outside these bands, and only an on-site visit produces a real estimate.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Project tier</th><th>Typical scope</th><th>Fixtures</th><th>Transformer</th><th>Planning range</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
