<tr><td>Medium</td><td>Full front yard with facade, trees, path, plus patio or back trees</td><td>14 to 24</td><td>300 W, or two 150 W, smart controller optional</td><td>4,500 to 9,500 dollars</td></tr>
<tr><td>Large</td><td>Whole property: facade, multiple trees, paths, patio, pool, steps, moonlighting, zoned smart control</td><td>25 to 50 or more</td><td>Two or three transformers, multi-zone smart system</td><td>9,500 to 20,000 dollars and up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Add-on zone</td><td>Extending an existing system to a new area</td><td>4 to 8</td><td>Uses existing capacity or adds a unit</td><td>1,200 to 3,000 dollars</td></tr>
<tr><td>Design only</td><td>Written plan with fixture schedule and wiring layout</td><td>n/a</td><td>n/a</td><td>300 to 1,200 dollars</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For a quick personalized estimate, the <a href="/tools/cost-estimator/">cost estimator</a> walks through fixture counts and features and returns a planning range for your property.</p>

<h2>What Drives Cost Up</h2>
<p>Several factors reliably push an Austin project toward the upper end of its tier.</p>
<p>Mature live oaks are the most common. A single multi-trunk live oak with a 50-foot canopy often needs three uplights cross-lighting the trunks plus one or two moonlights in the canopy, which is five fixtures for one tree. A cedar elm or cypress needs one.</p>
<p>Hill Country grade changes add steps, retaining walls, and terraces, each of which needs safety lighting. Hardscape lights under wall caps and recessed step lights cost more per unit than path lights and require more careful installation.</p>
<p>Hardscape crossings add boring. A front yard divided by a driveway and two sidewalks may need three bores. Large lots in Steiner Ranch or Circle C mean long cable runs, heavier gauge wire, and sometimes a second transformer to control voltage drop.</p>
<p>Smart controls add a few hundred to nearly a thousand dollars, but they also let you dim zones, satisfy HOA lights-off hours, and keep the design dark-sky friendly without manual effort. Pool and water feature lighting requires submersible fixtures and specialized wiring, which adds to both hardware and labor.</p>

<h2>What Keeps Cost Down</h2>
<p>Good design is the biggest cost control. A clear hierarchy with one hero element and restraint elsewhere needs fewer fixtures than a yard that tries to light everything. Twelve well-placed fixtures routinely outperform twenty scattered ones.</p>
<p>Phasing helps. Many Austin Landscape Lighting clients install the front yard first with a transformer sized for the whole property, then add the back patio or pool zone a year later. The 20 percent headroom rule for transformer sizing makes this easy, and the add-on zone range in the table above reflects what a second phase typically costs.</p>
<p>Existing infrastructure matters. A GFCI outlet in the right place, open beds instead of mulched rock, and a layout without driveway crossings all trim labor. Standard brass fixtures rather than custom finishes keep hardware cost predictable without sacrificing lifespan.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/professional-landscape-lighting-austin-1.webp" alt="Finished Austin front yard with uplit live oak, grazed limestone facade, and staggered path lights at night" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>A medium-tier front yard project: three uplights on the live oak, four grazing fixtures on the facade, six path lights, and two step lights on a 300 watt transformer.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>LED Operating Cost</h2>
<p>Running cost is small. A 20-fixture LED system averaging 6 watts per fixture draws 120 watts. At six hours per night, that is 0.72 kWh per day or about 263 kWh per year. At Austin Energy residential rates in the neighborhood of 13 to 15 cents per kWh including fees, the system costs roughly 35 to 40 dollars per year to operate. A comparable halogen system from fifteen years ago would have drawn 700 to 1,000 watts and cost 230 to 330 dollars per year.</p>
<p>LED lamps also last 40,000 hours or more, which at six hours a night is well past 15 years. Maintenance consists of an annual check of connections, aiming, and lens cleaning rather than lamp replacement. If you have an older halogen system, the <a href="/tools/led-savings-calculator/">LED savings calculator</a> shows how quickly an LED retrofit pays for itself.</p>

<h2>DIY vs Professional Cost</h2>
<p>A do-it-yourself installation with brass fixtures from a landscape supply house, a quality transformer, and proper cable typically costs 50 to 60 percent of the professional price in materials alone. The savings are real, but so are the trade-offs. Design, voltage drop calculations, hub wiring, boring under hardscape, and night aiming are where most DIY systems fall short, and fixing an undersized transformer or a dim far zone later often costs more than doing it right the first time. Big-box kits with aluminum or plastic fixtures cost far less upfront and usually need full replacement within three to five years in Austin conditions.</p>
<p>A middle path is to pay for a professional design and install it yourself, or to have a professional install the transformer, trunk lines, and hubs while you place the fixtures. Either approach captures most of the quality while trimming labor.</p>

<h2>What a Written Estimate Should Include</h2>
<p>Any estimate you receive should itemize fixture count by type, the brand and material of each fixture, lamp wattage and color temperature, transformer brand and capacity, cable gauge, control type, and labor. It should also state the fixture warranty, the workmanship warranty, and whether night aiming and a follow-up adjustment visit are included. Austin Landscape Lighting provides written estimates after a free on-site consultation, and every installation carries a 2-year workmanship warranty on top of manufacturer fixture warranties. Vague per-project pricing with no fixture schedule is a sign to keep shopping.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/professional-landscape-lighting-austin-3.webp" alt="Brass well light and uplight fixtures laid out on a limestone patio before installation" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Solid brass fixtures cost more per unit than aluminum but last 20 or more years in Austin heat, which lowers lifetime cost considerably.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Is Landscape Lighting Worth the Cost?</h2>
<p>Beyond curb appeal, landscape lighting delivers safety on steps and paths, a measurable deterrent to intrusion, and extended use of patios and outdoor kitchens through Austin's long warm season. Real estate agents consistently cite lit exteriors as a factor in showing appeal and photography. Against a 20-year fixture life and under 40 dollars a year in electricity, most homeowners find the planning ranges above reasonable for the value returned. For more detail on fixture counts behind these ranges, see <a href="/how-many-landscape-lights-do-i-need/">how many landscape lights do I need</a>.</p>

<p>Landscape lighting cost becomes concrete only when someone walks your property, counts the trees, measures the runs, and understands what you want to see at night. Austin Landscape Lighting offers that consultation free, with a night demonstration and a written estimate that itemizes every component. Call +1 (281) 704-7210 or <a href="/contact/">reach out online</a> to schedule, and we will give you a planning number you can trust.</p>
